To find deep, hidden Remote Code Execution (RCE) vulnerabilities that standard scanners and average security researchers miss, you must look beyond basic command separators. Elite bug hunters target obscure parsers, unexpected binary behaviors, logic flaws in how arguments are split, or alternative interpretation layers.
Below are 20 highly advanced methodologies, edge cases, and techniques for uncovering hidden RCE.
------------------------------
## I. Advanced Shell & Parameter Manipulation## 1. Internal Field Separator ($IFS) Permutations
WAFs regularly block spaces, but many researchers don't realize that standard bash uses $IFS as a default field splitter. If $IFS alone is blocked, you can use specialized variations to bypass filters.

* Technique: Use positional parameters or alternate sequence slicing.
* Payloads: $IFS$9, ${IFS}, or __text__$IFS__command__

## 2. Shell Brace Expansion
When spaces, $IFS, and special characters are stripped entirely, Unix shells allow you to group a command and its parameters inside a comma-separated brace block.

* Technique: The shell automatically unpacks the commas into standard command arguments.
* Payload: {echo,hello} or {sleep,10}

## 3. Hex/Octal Encoding via Unquoted Strings
If an application sanitizes alphabet characters or filters specific command strings like cat or id, you can feed the shell raw octal or hexadecimal values that bypass string-matching engines entirely.

* Technique: Pass the command via an unquoted printf wrapper.
* Payload: $(printf+'\x69\x64') (executes id)

## 4. Variable Concatenation & Slicing
Modern detection engines look for explicit strings like /etc/passwd or whoami. You can dynamically construct these words at runtime by slicing default environment variables or piecing empty variables together.

* Technique: Split forbidden strings using uninitialized variables.
* Payload: w${x}h${y}o${z}a${a}m${b}i or a=who;b=ami;$a$b

## 5. Wildcard Blobs for Path Obfuscation
When system paths or explicit binary names are blacklisted, Unix globbing (wildcards) can bypass path restrictions while still hitting the target system binary precisely.

* Technique: Replace characters with ? (matches any single character) or * (matches multiple).
* Payload: /???/w??a?? (triggers /usr/bin/whoami or /bin/whoami)

