## III. Logic Flaws & Parser Impedance Matches## 10. HTTP Parameter Pollution (HPP) Split Execution
Some application infrastructures use an upstream proxy that handles parameters differently than the backend application logic.

* Technique: Split your command injection across two identical parameter keys.
* Payload: ?code=who&code=ami (where the upstream validates who as safe, but the backend concatenates them into whoami).

## 11. Multibyte Character Encoding Confusion
Web Application Firewalls often evaluate requests strictly in standard ASCII or UTF-8 formats. If the backend application processes input using alternative character sets (like Shift-JIS or EUC-KR), you can smuggle shell characters.

* Technique: Use the first half of a multibyte character to consume a filtering quote or backslash character.
* Payload: Smuggling a %e5 byte to break strings.

## 12. Double URL/Hex Encoding for Inner Parsers
If the backend application passes your input through multiple internal microservices before it reaches the execution point, it may undergo multiple decoding iterations.

* Technique: Double-encode or triple-encode your shell separators.
* Payload: Tracing %253B (which decodes first to %3B, then to a literal ; right before execution).

## 13. TOCTOU (Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use) Parameters
An application might perform strict validation on your input parameter when the request first hits the application layer, but pull a refreshed value from a session, database, or cache right before processing.

* Technique: Modify the code concurrently across two asynchronous fast-requests to bypass the initial check while delivering the payload to the execution step.

