## IV. Protocol & Underlying Handler Exploitation## 14. Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) Evaluation
If the registration code parameter handles dynamic text generation, confirmation emails, or internal logging templates.

* Technique: Inject template engine syntax to escape the application sandboxing and run code via the language's native runtime environment.
* Payload: ${7*7} or {{7*7}} (Looking for a math output of 49 to confirm processing).

## 15. Insecure Deserialization via Encoded Tokens
If the parameter YOURCODE is decoded into an object structure rather than evaluated as a plain string, it may contain serialized application components.

* Technique: Alter the properties of the serialized structure to call internal native gadget chains that run system commands upon instantiation.

## 16. Local File Inclusion (LFI) to RCE via Log Poisoning
If the code parameter can be forced to throw predictable errors that get written directly to the application server's access logs.

* Technique: Inject executable server code (like <?php system($_GET['cmd']); ?>) into the parameter, causing it to log, and then use an inclusion flaw to execute that log file. [1, 2] 

## 17. Wrapper Protocol Exploitation
If the processing backend passes your input parameter straight into standard file-opening or streaming functions.

* Technique: Move away from standard paths and leverage built-in language protocols.
* Payload: Using php://input or data://text/plain;base64,... to stream executable data directly into the execution flow. [3] 

